# Current configuration options:
# These all have a flag equivalent as well. See help pages
#    complete_dir   - remote base directory that contains symlinks to data in 'data_dir' that is ready to download
#    data_dir       - actual location of files that are being download (rather than symlinks
#    remote_dl_dir  - directory to look in for files
#    local_dl_dir   - directory to download to
#    editor         - the editor you prefer to use
#    picker         - (tui|editor|[unset]) how to choose files: the terminal UI, $editor,
#                     or unset for the UI wherever the terminal supports it
#    listfile       - where to download list of available files to. Four more
#                     files are derived from it for one session's use: the data
#                     listing, the directory sizes, the long listing that says
#                     where each symlink points, and the data directory's own
#                     sizes, which is what the picker's STATUS column compares
#                     a local copy against
#    logfile        - where lftp mirror writes its transfer log
#    lockfile       - one run at a time; this file holds the running pid
#                     (default /tmp/alftp.lock)
#    record         - list of everything that has been successfully downloaded,
#                     one tab-separated "time profile entry status" line per entry
#                     (default ~/.cache/alftp/alftp.record)
#    errors         - the same, for entries that did not arrive
#                     (default ~/.cache/alftp/alftp.errors)
#    verify         - where checksum=True writes what it verified
#                     (default ~/.cache/alftp/alftp.verify)
#    server         - ssh server name
#    username       - ssh username
#    port           - ssh port
#    password       - ssh password. If there is no password, no keyfile and no
#                     gpg_file, alftp asks for one where there is a terminal to ask at
#    keyfile        - ssh private key for sftp key authentication, instead of a
#                     password (keyFile= is accepted too)
#    gpg_file       - path to a gpg-encrypted file of settings (typically just
#                     'password=...'), decrypted with `gpg --batch --decrypt` at
#                     startup and evaluated like the rest of the config, so no
#                     secret has to sit here in plaintext
#    autoUncompress - (True|False) automatically decompress what was downloaded.
#                     Default False; -nu turns it off for one run even where
#                     this is True. The kinds it knows are rar, zip, 7z, tar
#                     (including .tar.gz/.tgz, .tar.bz2/.tbz2, .tar.xz/.txz,
#                     .tar.zst/.tzst) and the single-file compressors gz, bz2,
#                     xz and zst. Each needs its own tool installed -- unrar,
#                     unzip, 7z, tar, gzip, bzip2, xz, zstd -- and a kind whose
#                     tool is missing is stepped over with a line to $errors.
#    uncompress_exclude
#                   - kinds autoUncompress must leave packed, named as above and
#                     separated by spaces or commas, e.g. "zip, 7z". Default
#                     empty, which unpacks every kind it can
#                     A downloaded directory is unpacked into itself and its
#                     archives are then deleted; an archive downloaded as an
#                     entry of its own is unpacked into a directory named after
#                     it and the archive is kept. Only the volume a set opens at
#                     is unpacked (.rar, or part01 of a .partNN.rar set) --
#                     unrar finds the rest of the set itself
#    checksum       - (True|False) after each download, write its size and (for a
#                     single file) its sha256 to $verify. Default False
#    chmod          - (True|False) apply perms_dirs/perms_files to what was
#                     downloaded. Default False: content keeps the permissions it
#                     arrived with. Whatever autoUncompress unpacked is walked
#                     by the same pass, so it gets these settings as well
#    perms_dirs     - three-digit mode for downloaded directories (default 744).
#                     Archives get this too: .rar and its .rNN parts, .zip, .7z,
#                     .tar and its compressed forms, .gz, .bz2, .xz. An archive
#                     holds other files rather than being one, so it counts as a
#                     directory here -- at the top level and at any depth inside
#                     a downloaded directory
#    perms_files    - three-digit mode for downloaded files (default 644)
#    chown          - (True|False) give what was downloaded to $owner. Default False
#    owner          - user, or user:group, for chown=True
#    prune_broken   - (True|False, default True) leave broken symlinks out of the listing and
#                     remove them from the remote. A link is broken when the data it points at is
#                     gone; one whose target cannot be checked is always kept. --dry-run only says
#                     what it would remove, and -do leaves the remote alone.
#    update_interval - (seconds, default 0) how often the terminal UI checks the
#                     remote for entries that have appeared or gone while it is
#                     open. 0 is never; "u" checks whatever this says. The check
#                     is a background login of its own, so the UI stays usable
#                     while it runs
#    confirm_quit    - (True|False, default True) ask before leaving the terminal
#                     UI. False quits on one keystroke in the one case where
#                     the question has nothing in it: nothing marked that has
#                     not already been transferred, nothing to unlink or
#                     delete, and no transfer still running. A quit that would
#                     throw work away asks whatever this is set to
#    line_numbers    - (True|False, default True) show a line-number gutter down
#                     the left of the terminal UI. Each row says how far it is
#                     from the cursor; the row under the cursor says which line
#                     it is, which is the number "G" takes
#    recursive_listing
#                   - (True|False, default True) list the whole data directory
#                     once, in the background, when the terminal UI opens.
#                     "tab" then opens a directory with no login of its own,
#                     and a directory has a real size instead of the size of
#                     its directory entry. "ls -R" is the command, with an
#                     automatic fallback to find plus "du -a -h" for a server
#                     that ignores -R. In srcs mode each source directory is
#                     walked when it is first opened, not all of them up front
#    concurrent_downloads
#                   - (count, default 2) how many transfers started from the
#                     terminal UI with "enter" run at once. Each one is a
#                     background login of its own; the rest wait in the queue,
#                     in the order the list has them
#    norm           - (True|[unset]) do not remove symlinks when completed
#    rm_remote      - (True|[unset]) remove files, not just symlinks, once completed
#    rm_list        - (True|[unset]) remove list file once download is complete
#    no_append      - (True|[unset]) do not append downloaded files to record#
# In alftp.src.conf, a profile block may also set per-profile download
# directories, which override the general dl_dir/local_dl_dir for one profile:
#    dl_dir_<profile>        - local directory for that profile only
#    remote_dl_dir_<profile> - remote directory for that profile only
# The <profile> suffix is part of a variable name, so characters outside
# A-Za-z0-9_ become '_' (profile "tv-shows" -> dl_dir_tv_shows). See README.md.
#
# A block may also name the source directories that live under complete_dir:
#    srcs=TV; music; books
# "alftp -i" with no profile name after it picks from complete_dir itself, with
# those srcs as the top of the tree; each one lands in whatever its own
# dl_dir_<src> -- or dl_dir with @profile standing for the src -- resolves to.
# It is the one key in the file that is not bash (the ";" would end the
# assignment), so it is read as text rather than eval'd, and cannot be set in
# this file.
#
# NOTE: alftp.src.conf is not a key=value file. Each profile block is eval'd as
# bash, which is what lets a block contain conditionals -- and also means the
# file is code, not inert data: anything in it runs with your privileges when
# alftp starts. Treat it the way you would treat ~/.bashrc, and do not paste
# profile blocks from anywhere you would not run a script from.
